    forEvidence: "None. The claim survives only as an unexamined assumption left behind by the slave system's own propaganda, which needed its victims to be 'people without history'.",
    againstEvidence: "The documented record: the kingdom of Kongo exchanged ambassadors and letters with Portugal and the Vatican from the 1480s (the correspondence survives); Mali's ruler Mansa Musa's 1324 pilgrimage is documented across Arab sources as legendary wealth; Timbuktu's manuscript libraries (hundreds of thousands of manuscripts, many now digitized) prove centuries of West African scholarship; Dahomey, Oyo, Benin, and Asante were sophisticated documented states (Benin's bronzes stunned Europe when looted in 1897). Shipping records tie Haitian and African-American ancestry to precisely these regions.",
    body: "Bottom line: the ancestors of the African diaspora came from documented kingdoms with courts, archives, universities, diplomacy, and art. The heritage wasn't absent — it was severed, deliberately. The difference matters totally: absent things cannot be recovered; severed things can. That recovery — through records, linguistics, genetics, and elder testimony — is the entire mission of the Roots & Recovery section and the Witness Project.",
    deep: "This claim is rarely spoken aloud anymore, but it does not need to be: it operates as an inherited assumption. It was manufactured deliberately — slave traders, planters, and their intellectual defenders needed Africans to be 'people without history' because the whole system's moral defense required it, and that propaganda outlived the system. It echoes in Hegel's notorious dismissal of Africa as outside history, in colonial-era textbooks, and today in the quiet way school curricula begin Black history at the slave ship, as though nothing stood behind the Atlantic's far shore. Grading it is easy; the point of this entry is to show how overwhelming the documentation on the other side actually is.\n\nStart with paper. The kingdom of Kongo entered into diplomatic and epistolary relations with Portugal from the 1480s, and the surviving correspondence includes the letters of King Afonso I (Nzinga Mbemba) in the early 1500s — a Central African monarch writing to his Portuguese counterpart, including protests against the slave trade's ravages in his own kingdom; Kongo also exchanged emissaries with the Vatican. In West Africa, the empire of Mali was famous across the medieval world: Mansa Musa's pilgrimage to Mecca in 1324 is documented in Arab sources, which describe gold spending so lavish it was remembered for generations; the traveler Ibn Battuta visited Mali in the 1350s and left an account. Timbuktu became a center of Islamic scholarship whose manuscript libraries — held today by institutions like the Ahmed Baba Institute and by family collections, with large digitization efforts ongoing — preserve centuries of African writing on law, theology, astronomy, and medicine. Further south and east stood documented states with courts, armies, taxation, and diplomacy: Benin, whose cast-bronze and brass artworks stunned European observers when British forces looted them in the 1897 punitive expedition; Oyo, Dahomey, and Asante, all known through African, European, and Arabic records. And the connection to the diaspora is not vague: the Trans-Atlantic Slave Trade Database at slavevoyages.org compiles records of more than thirty thousand documented voyages, tying the ancestry of Haitians, African Americans, Brazilians, and West Indians to precisely these coasts and their hinterlands.\n\nThere is no steel-man for the claim itself — no evidence supports it, which is what the verdict false means — but there is a steel-man for a neighboring truth that explains the claim's persistence: much was genuinely destroyed. The Middle Passage deliberately severed names, languages, lineages, and religions; enslavers suppressed literacy and broke up families as policy. Someone who says 'the records of my particular family are gone' is often, painfully, correct. But the entry's distinction carries the whole weight: absent and severed are different conditions. A heritage that never existed cannot be recovered; a heritage that was cut can be traced back across the cut — through shipping manifests, port records, African archives, linguistics, genetics, and elder testimony. The kingdoms were real, the documents survive, and the recovery work is not aspiration but method — which is exactly the mission of the Roots & Recovery section and the Witness Project.",
